t the things you think  about a lot right what are the limits is  everything knowable uh what determines  um no ability  and um and i think um you know i should  come clean i mean i already you know as  you know scientists fall into camps i  mean  i believe there is no reason to think  that everything ought to be knowable  [Laughter]  all right you're allowed to think that i  think that uh my i'm very happy  to imagine that not everything is  knowable but i'm much more impressed  with how much we have figured out than  we have any right to be so i'm  suspicious that you know i'm not i don't  think we're at the boundaries or the the  limits of any of those things anytime  soon but i wanted to ask i mean since  we're past the hour mark of the of the  podcast this is where we let our hair  down a little bit um  what about the sociology of the  community in the face of anomalies like  this i mean i had adam reese on the  podcast we talked about the hubble  tension uh we've talked in different uh  places about different kinds of  contentious scientific issues and  you know i think that there is a  a tendency  among non-scientists to think of the  scientific community as much more  monolithic as it is either they're all  you know pushing the agenda of the  establishment or whatever or or they're  all  blown geniuses with their own theories  but really there's a lot of  heterogeneity right like when you have a  claim like this that there's an anomaly  we should be paying attention to  some people are going to be like oh yeah  we should pay attention to that others  like ah it'll go away  what is your feeling about the different  camps out there in the community yeah i  mean  it's so acutely sean unsurprisingly  right so i mean the community is really  quite um  heterogeneous in terms of their  attitudes  and uh and i think that there is a large  portion of the community that is very  risky averse  and that is kind of you know all these  this heterogeneity kind of mirrors t